WEBVTT

00:00:08.400 --> 00:00:13.600
That's the first line of a book nobody typed by hand, and nobody read aloud.

00:00:13.950 --> 00:00:20.250
Well, nearly. A person decides what happens. Claude writes it, and a voice running on a laptop reads it.

00:00:21.100 --> 00:00:28.050
This is the Fantasy Audiobook Studio. It writes short fantasy novels, and turns them into audiobooks.

00:00:28.400 --> 00:00:33.450
Each book lives in one folder. Chapters, notes, names, and the finished audio.

00:00:33.800 --> 00:00:40.975
The work is split into skills. A skill is a set of written instructions that Claude follows for one job.

00:00:41.825 --> 00:00:47.325
The skills run in a fixed order, called the pipeline. Eleven stages, from zero to ten.

00:00:48.175 --> 00:00:55.675
Stages zero to five are writing. An idea becomes a story bible, an outline, chapters, and then edits.

00:00:56.525 --> 00:01:04.025
Stages six to nine are the voice. The text is fixed for the ear, turned into a script, narrated, and checked by a listener.

00:01:04.875 --> 00:01:09.750
Stage ten packages it into an audiobook, and publishes the website.

00:01:10.600 --> 00:01:17.900
Story seed comes first. It turns a one-line idea into choices. Twists, heroes and endings. The author picks.

00:01:18.750 --> 00:01:26.650
Fantasy novella is the hub. It knows the order of the work, keeps the files tidy, and writes one chapter at a time.

00:01:27.500 --> 00:01:35.075
Fiction workshop is a team of editors. It builds the story bible, edits, and tests the book on a reader who has never seen it.

00:01:35.925 --> 00:01:42.350
Story structure places the big turning points, so the middle doesn't sag and the ending lands.

00:01:43.200 --> 00:01:49.100
Character archetypes checks that every important character has a clear job, and isn't a cliché.

00:01:49.950 --> 00:01:57.450
Prose mechanics is a proofreader for style. Repeated words, weak verbs, and sentences that all sound the same.

00:01:58.300 --> 00:02:04.975
Avoid AI writing removes the habits that give machine text away. No tapestries. No testaments.

00:02:05.825 --> 00:02:13.650
Speech flow fixes the text for the ear. A listener can't see commas or italics, so the words have to do that work.

00:02:14.500 --> 00:02:21.650
Narration prep turns each chapter into a script for the voice. Names are spelled out by sound, and pauses are written in.

00:02:22.500 --> 00:02:29.675
Kokoro narrate is the narrator. A free voice model that runs on this laptop, with no cloud service.

00:02:30.525 --> 00:02:38.575
Listen check is a robot proof-listener. It transcribes the audio, compares it with the script, and reports a short list of timestamps.

00:02:39.425 --> 00:02:46.000
And audiobook assemble evens out the volume, adds chapters and a cover, and builds the final file.

00:02:46.850 --> 00:02:56.150
The studio also learns. When the author hears something wrong, Claude finds the cause, and renders a before and an after. The author picks by ear.

00:02:56.500 --> 00:03:04.450
The winning fix becomes a rule in a skill, with a test. The tests run before every publish, so an old fix can't quietly break.

00:03:04.800 --> 00:03:12.975
And the automatic listener finds problems nobody reported. A garbled two-word line in book one was found that way.

00:03:13.325 --> 00:03:19.900
When the same kind of fix turns up twice, Claude drafts the rule. Whether it sounds right is still the author's call.

00:03:20.750 --> 00:03:25.100
And when Claude says a book is finished, that isn't taken on trust.

00:03:25.450 --> 00:03:31.875
Every stage's script writes a line to a log as it runs. What it read, what it wrote, and whether it finished.

00:03:32.225 --> 00:03:41.850
Then a checker goes through the stages. It proves that the audiobook was made from today's script, and that the script was made from today's manuscript.

00:03:42.200 --> 00:03:46.950
Edit one word, and the checker shows exactly which stages have to run again.

00:03:47.800 --> 00:03:56.525
One moment from the intake scene. Mira works in the afterlife's paperwork office. She is telling a dead dentist he will become a teapot.

00:03:56.875 --> 00:04:02.750
The outline gave chapter one its job. A hook in the first line, then a routine day at the desk.

00:04:03.600 --> 00:04:07.800
She asks if he wants to appeal. He answers with two words.

00:04:08.150 --> 00:04:11.750
On the page, that works. Listen to what the voice did with it.

00:04:12.500 --> 00:04:14.300
♪ "You want to appeal it?"

00:04:15.250 --> 00:04:16.775
♪ "Can I?"

00:04:18.425 --> 00:04:22.525
It sounds cut off, more like half a sentence than a question.

00:04:22.875 --> 00:04:29.275
The voice model gave both words weak stress. With nothing stressed, there's nothing for the question to lean on.

00:04:30.125 --> 00:04:36.800
The first fix went into the writing. Give the question a beat after it, so the listener hears the feeling.

00:04:37.150 --> 00:04:41.950
That helped, but the question was still weak. The beat doesn't give the words any stress.

00:04:42.800 --> 00:04:51.950
So the script does the rest. It stresses the first word, doubles the question mark, and lets the question stand alone, with the beat after it.

00:04:52.700 --> 00:04:54.200
♪ "Can I??"

00:04:55.150 --> 00:04:56.925
♪ He sat up a little.

00:04:58.075 --> 00:05:06.950
The author tried five versions and picked this one by ear. Now narration prep does it for every short question, and listen check confirms the stress.

00:05:07.800 --> 00:05:16.350
From there, narration prep spells Mira's name by sound. Kokoro reads it, listen check compares, and assemble builds the book.

00:05:25.500 --> 00:05:30.250
Every book, every skill, and every fix, with the audio, is on the website.

00:05:30.600 --> 00:05:32.375
Thanks for listening.
